.TH GHGREP 1 "September 2026" "ghgrep 2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ghgrep \- smart group-header grep: search with group context

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ghgrep
[OPTIONS]
.I PATTERN
[FILE...]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ghgrep
searches text for lines matching a regular expression, like
.BR grep (1),
but for each match it also prints the "group header" that the matching line belongs to \- e.g. a
.RI [ section ]
heading, a line following a
.B ----
separator, or (by default) a heuristically-detected header line.

If no FILE is given, or FILE is
.BR \- ,
input is read from standard input.

.SH HEADER DETECTION
Headers are detected in this priority order:
.TP
.B 1.
An explicit \-\-header\-pattern, \-\-separator, or \-\-brackets given on the command line.
.TP
.B 2.
Bracketed lines: [something]
.TP
.B 3.
A separator line (----, ====, ****, ...) marks the following non-empty line as a header.
.TP
.B 4.
A non-indented line immediately followed by an indented line.
.TP
.B 5.
A non-indented, non-empty line immediately following a blank line.
.TP
.B 6.
A non-indented line containing a metadata-like marker (pid:, PID:, exe, Name:, Process).

Each header starts a new group; a group runs until the next header or end of file. If no headers are
detected at all, the whole input is treated as a single headerless group.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-i, \-\-ignore\-case
Case-insensitive matching.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-invert\-match
Invert the sense of matching for group selection: a line "matches" if it does
.B not
match PATTERN. A group is included if it has at least one matching line by this rule; an included group
is still printed in full (subject to \-\-context / \-\-only\-headers), so an originally-matching line can
still appear if its group also contains non-matching lines.
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-line\-number
Prefix each printed line with its 1-based line number.
.TP
.B \-H, \-\-with\-filename
Prefix each printed line with its file name. Enabled automatically when more than one FILE is given.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-filename
Never prefix lines with the file name.
.TP
.B \-C, \-\-context N
After a header, also print the following N lines (in addition to the matching lines and one line of
context around each match). If omitted, the whole group is printed.
.TP
.B \-\-header\-pattern REGEX
Use REGEX to decide which lines are headers, instead of the smart heuristic.
.TP
.B \-\-separator STR
Treat a line consisting solely of one or more repetitions of STR as a separator; the next non-empty line
after it is a header.
.TP
.B \-\-brackets
Treat lines of the form [something] as headers.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-smart
Disable the heuristic header detector (used when none of \-\-header\-pattern / \-\-separator /
\-\-brackets apply); with this and no other detector selected, no headers are found.
.TP
.B \-\-only\-headers
Print only the headers of matching groups, not the matching detail lines.
.TP
.B \-\-color
Colorize headers and matching lines with ANSI escapes. Enabled automatically when stdout is a terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show a brief usage summary and exit.

.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.B ghgrep "System32" handles.txt
.RE
.RS
.B ghgrep \-i "listening" netstat.txt
.RE
.RS
.B ghgrep \-\-brackets "ESTABLISHED" ports.txt
.RE
.RS
.B ghgrep \-\-separator="----" "SysWOW64" handles.txt
.RE
.RS
.B ghgrep \-\-header\-pattern '^\e[.*\e]$' "1042" ports.txt
.RE

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, even if a named file was missing/unreadable or no lines matched (a diagnostic is printed to
stderr for each such file).
.TP
.B 1
PATTERN is not a valid regular expression.

.SH AUTHOR
Chris Pollitt.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR grep (1),
.BR re (7)
